```cpp
std::string readStringWcharTag(BasicIo::UniquePtr& io, size_t length) {
  Internal::enforce(length <= io->size() - io->tell(), Exiv2::ErrorCode::kerCorruptedMetadata);
  DataBuf FieldBuf(length + 1);
  io->readOrThrow(FieldBuf.data(), length, ErrorCode::kerFailedToReadImageData);
  std::wstring wst(FieldBuf.begin(), FieldBuf.end());
  return utf16ToUtf8(wst);
}
```

The `std::wstring wst(FieldBuf.begin(), FieldBuf.end())` - this constructs a wstring from the byte range. But `FieldBuf` contains bytes, not wide characters. The `wstring` constructor from iterators will interpret each byte as a `wchar_t`. On a 64-bit system, `wchar_t` is 4 bytes (32 bits). So if `FieldBuf` has `length + 1` bytes, the wstring constructor will read `(length + 1) / 4` wchar_t values, but it reads 4 bytes at a time. If `length + 1` is not a multiple of 4, the last read will go past the end of the buffer.

