For example, if `length = 3`, then `FieldBuf` has 4 bytes. The wstring constructor reads 4 bytes at a time (sizeof(wchar_t) = 4 on Linux), so it reads exactly 4 bytes which is fine. But if `length = 2`, then `FieldBuf` has 3 bytes. The wstring constructor reads 4 bytes at a time, so it reads 4 bytes from a 3-byte buffer, causing a 1-byte heap-buffer-overflow.

But the issue says READ 8. On macOS, `wchar_t` is 4 bytes too. On Windows, `wchar_t` is 2 bytes. Hmm.

Actually, let me reconsider. The `std::wstring` constructor from iterators: `template<class InputIt> basic_string(InputIt first, InputIt last, const Allocator& alloc = Allocator())`. This constructs the string from the range `[first, last)`. For each element in the range, it reads one `wchar_t` value. The iterator `FieldBuf.begin()` is a `byte*` iterator, and `FieldBuf.end()` is also a `byte*` iterator. So the range is `[begin, end)` which has `length + 1` bytes. The constructor reads `wchar_t` values from this range, reading `sizeof(wchar_t)` bytes at a time. If `length + 1` is not a multiple of `sizeof(wchar_t)`, the last read will go past the end.

On Linux, `sizeof(wchar_t)` is 4. So if `length + 1` is not a multiple of 4, we get a heap-buffer-overflow READ of up to 3 bytes past the end. But the issue says READ 8.

